WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.640
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on to the next item which is General Practice Services programme

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:18.080
update.  Welcome Ros Roughton, Director of NHS Commissioning, to join us for the paper.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.860
Bruce I think you are going to kick us off

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.720
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think this is 
a

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:30.289
really important paper, and I think it is
important because just under a year ago, we

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:37.280
published the Five Year Forward View for General
Practice. We commission general practice,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:43.720
which is frankly the backbone of our NHS and
has been since its inception, but since then,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:49.670
a number of things have changed. Firstly,
the amount of knowledge that general practitioners

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:56.250
are meant to have has reached the level that
we previously thought consultant general physicians

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:03.229
should have. Secondly, the patients have become
far more complex. Thirdly, there has been

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:09.920
longstanding underfunding of primary care,
which this Board has successfully started

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:16.119
to reverse. All of this is happening at a
time that we are trying to treat more patients

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:21.670
at home and keep them out of hospital, in
the face of an ageing population with multiple

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:29.740
ailments of old age. This puts primary care
staff - and I do not just mean general practitioners,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:36.900
but I mean the full gamut of people who work
in primary care - under frankly relentless

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:43.049
pressure, and they feel it on a day-by-day
basis. This paper outlines some of the progress

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:48.930
that we have made since the publication of
the Five Year Forward View in General Practice

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:54.520
to relieving some of those pressures, and
also outlines some of our ambitions. I would

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.619
like to pay particular tribute to Ros, who
has done a huge amount of work on this in

00:01:58.619 --> 00:02:03.689
terms of engaging with general practitioners,
particularly the General Practice Committee

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:07.409
of the BMA and the Royal College of General
Practitioners.

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:15.610
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: I am delighted to present
to the Board an update on our work. As a reminder,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:20.810
when we published the General Practice Forward
View last April, it really was designed to

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.760
do three things. Firstly, it was to mark a
turning point for general practice by recognising

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.370
the pressures that it has been under, and
the relative underinvestment compared to other

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:35.830
sectors. Secondly, stabilising general practice
under pressure, through offering some very

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:43.760
practical support here and now. Thirdly, stimulating
and supporting general practice to transform,

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:50.910
recognising that the nature of healthcare
and health conditions, and people who they

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:57.280
are dealing with, are different from 70 years
ago. This paper sets out for the record all

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:02.500
the work underway over the last 11 months,
and it also includes some case studies which

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:07.489
I hope give a flavour of what this means in
practice on the ground, both for practices

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:08.569
and for patients.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:14.050
I would like to particularly pull out three
things. I think first, just on investment,

00:03:14.050 --> 00:03:22.849
it is worth noting that we are on track in
2016/2017 for putting an extra £0.5 billion

00:03:22.849 --> 00:03:26.590
into general practice services compared to
the year before.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:33.060
Secondly, on workforce: I think for the medical
workforce, I would say the last year we have

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:39.560
been particularly focusing on how we can improve
return-to-work processes. So, working with

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:45.079
the Royal College of GPs and Health Education
England, we have really transformed how easy

00:03:45.079 --> 00:03:50.920
it is for a doctor to come back and work in
the English general practice system. We have

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:56.020
a single point of contact, financial support
and streamlined assessment routes. When we

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:01.769
started on this work, I think we had about
seven people registered to want to come back.

00:04:01.769 --> 00:04:08.060
When we looked last month, we had over 350
doctors, so it has been a really significant

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.060
transformation.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:14.950
It is far more than just the medical workforce.
We are keen to support the development of

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:19.890
multi-disciplinary teams, and this year we
have been rolling out clinical pharmacists

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:25.570
in general practice, and we are beginning
to see the benefits of that; not just in terms

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:31.920
of practice workload but in the outcomes for
patients. We are trying to capture that so

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.860
we understand the key ingredients for, when
you are bringing a pharmacist into general

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:42.010
practice, to make the most of that expertise.
We will be working with the Chief Nursing

00:04:42.010 --> 00:04:47.880
Officer about measures we can take to support
general practice nursing: another key part

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.140
of our workforce.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:56.230
The final area I would like to talk about
is about the redesign of care in general practice.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:01.800
I think many people have talked to us about
the need not just to stabilise general practice,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.230
but to support practices in taking the next
step for transformation. There is real bottom-up

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:14.380
momentum out there, particularly around practices
coming together to work at scale, around using

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:21.480
the wider workforce, around working out how
general practice can enable self-care, and

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:27.040
using the latest technology. We are really
delighted to be supporting that bottom-up

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:31.720
movement out there with the National General
Practice Development Programme. We have 86

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:36.900
schemes now registered, so this is practices
that have come to us to say they want to do

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:44.470
stuff, and that covers about 107 CCG areas
in the country. This programme highlights

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:49.150
things like the ten high-impact actions that
practices can take, and we offer support to

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:53.410
them in taking those steps, which help improve
the experience of patients.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:58.940
I think earlier this month, we held an event
with about 500 participants from practices,

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:03.780
local medical committees, patient groups and
commissioners from all around the country.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:08.710
This was not about NHS England saying, 'These
are the things you can do,' this was about

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:14.390
local places and local people talking to other
local places and local people about changes

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:17.900
that they have made that have made a difference,
and how they have done it, and what has made

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:19.300
a difference.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:26.170
We heard from places talking about how they
have trained up staff, care navigators, so

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:32.780
people who can help patients access all the
community and voluntary-sector groups out

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:38.430
there, and we are seeing for those cohorts
of people reductions in their use of general

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:43.330
practice time, in their use of A&amp;E, in their
use of public-sector resources, and happier

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.230
people.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:51.650
We heard work about redesign of workflow within
practices, so we heard from practices talking

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:56.800
about measures they take, and that we are
freeing up around 45 minutes per doctor per

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:04.160
day in their practices, so really concrete
things that we hope will free up GP time to

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:11.070
be able to support the most complex patients
and improve access then for the whole body

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.190
of patients.

00:07:12.190 --> 00:07:17.050
Thirdly, we heard about ways in which practices
can work together at scale, and what the point

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:23.601
of working together at scale is. I think one
of the key things coming out about scale from

00:07:23.601 --> 00:07:29.710
our general practice networks is about the
difference that is making to staff retention

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:38.250
and staff development, so the ability to employ
a wider range of disciplines across a bigger

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:44.050
population, and that makes for a more attractive
career, creates better resilience in practices,

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.500
and helps to improve patient experience.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.440
I think what is exciting is how much energy
there is out there to take forward changes,

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:54.841
but what general practice needs is support
to do that, and that is why, in the General

00:07:54.841 --> 00:08:00.340
Practice Forward View, we continue in the
year to come in making sure that we continue

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:05.690
to increase investment, but increase that
in a way which supports the kind of changes

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:11.180
that we need to see for the future, and that
in turn I hope will help us to improve access

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:18.270
of patients to general practice, which is
the key number-one piece of feedback that

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:25.150
we get from the public about what differences
they want to see in the years to come.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:35.390
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I am going to speak on behalf
of NHS Digital, if that is okay, Ros? Enabling

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:40.629
this programme with the technology strategy
that you pick up in your document is one of

00:08:40.629 --> 00:08:46.379
the three major priorities for NHSD this year,
so it has definitely put its shoulder to the

00:08:46.379 --> 00:08:51.910
wheel, as it were. One of the most important
elements is the ability to share records across

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:59.350
different patient settings into care settings
and into community pharmacy, etc. Could you

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:05.470
just say a little bit about what you think
the benefits to patients will be of a more

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:09.800
open way of using this technology to share
patient records?

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:16.600
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: I think very practically,
if we talk about extended access, so patients

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:22.721
being able to get access to GP appointments
at weekends, which is something that we are

00:09:22.721 --> 00:09:29.699
very committed to doing, what we know is it
would be wrong to expect every practice to

00:09:29.699 --> 00:09:36.170
be open every day of the week, so what we
are looking at creating are extended access

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:42.829
hubs. What we know from past experience of
improving access in general practice is that

00:09:42.829 --> 00:09:49.160
if a patient goes to a place that does not
have access to their record, they then cannot,

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:55.379
as it were, have perhaps the referral on,
if that is what is needed; they may simply

00:09:55.379 --> 00:10:00.920
be dealt with as an urgent thing and then
told to go and see their normal GP or their

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:07.410
normal practice during the week. Access to
the record, obviously with all the appropriate

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:14.389
governance and consent around that, will mean
that patients, if they go and see a practice

00:10:14.389 --> 00:10:18.980
or an extended access hub at the weekend,
can be dealt with fully in that one episode

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:24.700
rather than needing to return again into the
NHS later, so that is a very practical thing

00:10:24.700 --> 00:10:27.089
where I think that would help.

00:10:27.089 --> 00:10:33.770
&gt;&gt;Michelle Mitchell: Thanks, Ros, a really
important piece of work. Could you just give

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:38.860
some highlights or examples of, as a consequence
of the change in activity that has happened

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:44.060
in a number of areas, what have been the improvements
for patients and citizens that you yourself

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.060
have seen?

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:53.079
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: I think one of the areas that
has really been bottom-up is about how practices

00:10:53.079 --> 00:10:59.779
connect with the wider community, and particularly
with voluntary-sector groups. What we have

00:10:59.779 --> 00:11:06.769
seen there is patients who were perhaps coming
into general practice maybe every two or three

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:16.010
weeks, where really, general practice with
a sort of biomedical model was not the answer

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:25.370
for what they needed. So actually, being able
to support those patients in finding the other

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:30.230
things that were in the system that could
help them has been really important, and what

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:38.360
we have seen is that then those patients not
really needing the NHS, but actually being

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:45.660
able to start supporting themselves, and that
in turn leading to them feeling much more

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:51.029
positive about their potential and ability
to achieve what they want to achieve with

00:11:51.029 --> 00:11:53.870
their lives.

00:11:53.870 --> 00:12:01.399
One of the things that we have also seen in
that is actually supporting patients in the

00:12:01.399 --> 00:12:05.800
practice to become those practice champions,
so almost building up the general practice

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:10.550
not just as the staff who work in it, but
actually the members of the practice, so you

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:16.949
get the self-generated support across all
the people who are using that service. I think

00:12:16.949 --> 00:12:22.160
if we then think about the new models of care,
if you see general practice in some parts

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:28.920
of the country can be a natural hub for population-based
healthcare, but actually, that is not going

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.690
to work unless you have the wider community
sector involved.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:41.720
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Ros, the GP Five Year Forward
View was an immensely important strategy for

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:48.170
us, so I think it is really illuminating to
see how it is rolling out. If you were doing

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:52.939
a self-assessment of our success over the
first year, what mark would you give us out

00:12:52.939 --> 00:12:53.939
of ten?

00:12:53.939 --> 00:13:03.769
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: Well, I have very high standards,
Chairman, so I would probably say eight out

00:13:03.769 --> 00:13:12.439
of ten, in that I think we have changed the
mind-set about, actually, what we need to

00:13:12.439 --> 00:13:19.819
do is make things happen in general practice,
pay attention to it. I met with the BMA yesterday

00:13:19.819 --> 00:13:26.519
and I met representatives from LMCs all across
the country, and I think the debate that we

00:13:26.519 --> 00:13:31.879
are having is: how can we go faster, how can
we make more of a difference? What I hope

00:13:31.879 --> 00:13:36.899
is, we have shifted the mentality a bit more
to being: how do we take control of this,

00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:42.460
to shape the service that we want? Because
as we all know, everybody who comes to work

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:46.959
in general practice is trying to do a good
job and a great job for their community, and

00:13:46.959 --> 00:13:51.709
I think that what I hope we are beginning
to see is that we are trying to support them

00:13:51.709 --> 00:13:53.470
in doing that.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:14:00.869
There is still work to do, and we have done
a real assessment about the areas where we

00:14:00.869 --> 00:14:07.629
think we can improve next year, and are putting
steps in place to make sure that on some elements

00:14:07.629 --> 00:14:14.470
of the programme where we have not gone as
fast as we would like, that we start on 1st

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:20.529
April 2017 with really tackling those issues,
so that we can deliver fully in 2017/2018.

00:14:20.529 --> 00:14:26.740
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay, thanks for that, and
obviously, we will keep a close eye on that,

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:31.119
and assure ourselves. I think full marks to
you and your team for what has been achieved.

00:14:31.119 --> 00:14:38.040
Likewise, I detect that change in mood music
around general practice, and a belief that

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:43.839
the additional investment and the additional
interest that we are paying to general practice

00:14:43.839 --> 00:14:49.040
is starting to be understood and bringing
about the change that is needed.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:57.300
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Ros, I think we all accept
that significant underinvestment in general

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.680
practice has been going on for a number of
years, and therefore, it is a number of years

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:07.559
to correct that. I think you and your team,
as Malcolm said, have done an outstanding

00:15:07.559 --> 00:15:15.139
job in getting a lot done in a short period
of time. The delivery plan we know is going

00:15:15.139 --> 00:15:20.129
to have a major focus on general practice
in its broadest sense.

00:15:20.129 --> 00:15:27.009
I suppose just an observation as much as anything,
which is: certainly, the Board is completely

00:15:27.009 --> 00:15:34.009
focused on making sure that you have what
you need to be able to execute, and therefore,

00:15:34.009 --> 00:15:41.050
I am interested not just in the balance of
investment, but also in the balance of: the

00:15:41.050 --> 00:15:47.649
45 minutes to an hour a day, which is precious
time; how do we get that promulgated around

00:15:47.649 --> 00:15:56.839
the system as a whole? How do we help hard-pressed
GPs to have the time and the resource to be

00:15:56.839 --> 00:16:03.230
able to implement those changes? Because obviously,
it takes time to make change, to make things

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:10.199
better for them, so do we have the right support
package in place to help practices improve,

00:16:10.199 --> 00:16:13.749
get better, free up time, to allow more time
for patients?

00:16:13.749 --> 00:16:18.050
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: I think when we think about
this, we think about three ingredients you

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:24.389
need. You need will, so that is convincing
people that this is something that they want

00:16:24.389 --> 00:16:32.240
to do and will work, and I think that that
is not everywhere yet. For some of these things,

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:36.059
there is quite a sceptical audience, so actually
working on the, 'This can make a difference

00:16:36.059 --> 00:16:40.929
to you,' and that requires peer-to-peer conversation,
is by far and away the most effective.

00:16:40.929 --> 00:16:47.220
The second is about ideas, and I think that
we have now collected tens if not hundreds

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:52.410
- not quite hundreds - of case studies that
we are working through both from a clinical

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:57.779
patient experience and economic business perspective
about how that translates into a difference,

00:16:57.779 --> 00:17:01.329
to make sure these are ideas that are really
grounded in evidence.

00:17:01.329 --> 00:17:08.880
The third is execution, so I think that in
terms of execution capability, the general

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:14.580
practice sector has a longer way to go than
other sectors.

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:17.900
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: It is really tough if you
are running your own business, you are seeing

00:17:17.900 --> 00:17:22.790
patients, you have all those demands, so I
would encourage us to think about what type

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:29.780
of support we can make available. It is will
and skill, and time, and actually how we can

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:36.150
help people to do that. I think as Malcolm
said, you should be demanding of us to give

00:17:36.150 --> 00:17:37.320
you the support that you need.

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:40.660
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: I think one thing that is
important is that, in everything that we do,

00:17:40.660 --> 00:17:46.200
when we are thinking about leadership development
or any of the broader initiatives that we

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:50.940
do, that we think about the general practice
sector as being part of that, not separate.

00:17:50.940 --> 00:17:56.140
I think that is one of the things that has
shifted, but there is still a way to go. When

00:17:56.140 --> 00:18:02.180
I think about management trainees in the NHS,
they have tended previously to go to the trust

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:09.120
sector, so I think that if we want to see
a general practice sector that has the capabilities

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:17.370
to do the kind of improvements that the public
need and practices want, then we need to mainstream

00:18:17.370 --> 00:18:23.820
more some of our broader efforts, and when
we talk about the provider sector, we need

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:28.590
to be thinking general practice as well, and
I think those are things that we have moved

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:29.950
in the last 12 months.

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:34.360
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: And general practice not
being just doctors, but also nurses, practice

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:35.360
managers, etc.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:40.410
&gt;&gt;Ros Roughton: Absolutely, yes. I think what
we are seeing - to go back to Michelle's question

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:45.870
- is actually seeing the people registered
with general practice as part of the workforce,

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:46.870
almost.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:56.330
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Ros, very much
for the presentation, and may we just wish

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:58.330
you well for the next year of delivery.

